  VI. THE FORMULA IN RAPIDITY
  ----------------------------------------

  The correction for gap 2g:
  C_{2g}/C_2 = prod_{p|g, p odd} (p-1)/(p-2)

  In rapidity:
  ln(C_{2g}/C_2) / 2 = sum_{p|g, p odd} rapidity(f(p-2))
  = sum_{p|g, p odd} ln((p-1)/(p-2)) / 2
  = sum_{p|g, p odd} arctanh(1/(2p-3))

  Wait: f(p-2) = (p-1)/(p-2). arctanh of the ADDRESS of f(p-2):
  address of f(p-2) = (f-1)/(f+1) = ((p-1)/(p-2) - 1)/((p-1)/(p-2) + 1)
  = (1/(p-2)) / ((2p-3)/(p-2))
  = 1/(2p-3).

  So: rapidity of the correction = sum_{p|g, p odd} arctanh(1/(2p-3)).

  For p=3: arctanh(1/3) = ln(2)/2 = one octave. CHECK!
  For p=5: arctanh(1/7) = ln(4/3)/2 = one fourth. CHECK!
  For p=7: arctanh(1/11) = ln(6/5)/2 = one minor third. CHECK!

  THE CORRECTION RAPIDITY = SUM OF arctanh(1/(2p-3))
  OVER ODD PRIMES DIVIDING g.

  And 1/(2p-3) is the Cayley velocity for a SHIFTED prime:
  2p-3 = 3, 7, 11, 15, 19, 23, ...
  These are 2p-3 for p = 3, 5, 7, 9, 11, 13, ...
  Not all of these are prime (15 = 3*5, etc.).
  But the FIRST three are: 3, 7, 11 = OUR PALEY PRIMES!

  The correction for p=3 uses velocity 1/3 = address of 2 (octave).
  The correction for p=5 uses velocity 1/7 = address of 4/3 (fourth).
  The correction for p=7 uses velocity 1/11 = address of 6/5 (minor third).

  The velocities 1/3, 1/7, 1/11 are the Cayley addresses of
  the MUSICAL INTERVALS octave, fourth, minor third.
  And 3, 7, 11 are our fundamental primes from tournament theory!


  VII. THE TWIN PRIME CONSTANT IN RAPIDITY
  ----------------------------------------

  The twin prime constant C_2 = 2 * prod_{p>=3} p(p-2)/(p-1)^2.

  C_2 = 1.3203365930 (computed to p=10000)

  In rapidity:
  ln(C_2)/2 = ln(2)/2 + sum_{p>=3} ln(p(p-2)/(p-1)^2) / 2
  = 0.138943

  Each prime p contributes ln(p(p-2)/(p-1)^2)/2 to the twin prime rapidity.
  p(p-2)/(p-1)^2 = 1 - 1/(p-1)^2.
  ln(1 - 1/(p-1)^2) ~ -1/(p-1)^2 for large p.
  So the sum converges (sum of 1/p^2 converges).

  The twin prime constant's rapidity = ln(2)/2 + sum of small corrections.
  = one octave + a convergent correction from all primes.
  The correction is NEGATIVE (each factor < 1).
  So C_2 ~ 2 * (product of values < 1) < 2.
  C_2 = 1.320337 < 2. The octave is SHARPENED by the primes.


  VIII. THE COMPLETE FORMULA
  ----------------------------------------

  Density of prime pairs with gap 2g near N:

  pi_{2g}(N) ~ C_2 * prod_{p|g, p odd} f(p-2) * N / (ln N)^2

  = (twin prime constant)
    * (product of musical intervals from primes dividing g)
    * N / (ln N)^2

  In words: the number of prime pairs with gap 2g up to N is
  proportional to the CHORD VOLUME of the gap's prime factors,
  scaled by the twin prime constant and the square of the rapidity of N.

  The CHORD of gap 2g = product of musical intervals f(p-2) for p | g.
  Richer chords (more prime factors) = more common gaps.
  Unison chords (g = power of 2) = baseline twin prime density.


  IX. THE PRIMORIAL GAPS: MAXIMUM RESONANCE
  ----------------------------------------

  The primorial p# = product of all primes up to p.
  Its half-gap g = p#/2 is divisible by all odd primes up to p.
  So its chord includes ALL intervals up to f(p-2).

  p = 3: g = 3, gap = 6
    Chord: octave
    Correction: 2.0000
    This gap is 2.0x as common as twins.

  p = 5: g = 15, gap = 30
    Chord: octave * fourth
    Correction: 2.6667
    This gap is 2.7x as common as twins.

  p = 7: g = 105, gap = 210
    Chord: octave * fourth * minor third
    Correction: 3.2000
    This gap is 3.2x as common as twins.

  p = 11: g = 1155, gap = 2310
    Chord: octave * fourth * minor third * minor whole tone
    Correction: 3.5556
    This gap is 3.6x as common as twins.

  p = 13: g = 15015, gap = 30030
    Chord: octave * fourth * minor third * minor whole tone * undecimal neutral second
    Correction: 3.8788
    This gap is 3.9x as common as twins.

  The primorial gaps get RICHER and RICHER chords.
  They are the gaps that resonate with EVERY prime.
  They are the MOST COMMON gaps (relative to their size).

  Gap 210 = 2*3*5*7 = primorial(7):
  Chord = octave * fourth * minor third
  = 2 * 4/3 * 6/5 = 48/15 = 16/5 = 3.2.
  This gap is 3.2x as common as twin primes.

  Gap 30030 = 2*3*5*7*11*13 = primorial(13):
  Chord correction: 3.8788
  = 3.9x as common as twins.


  X. WHY GAP 6 IS THE MOST COMMON SMALL GAP
  ----------------------------------------

  Empirical observation: gap 6 (sexy primes) occurs more often
  than gap 2 (twin primes) in practice.

  Up to 1000000:
  Gap 2 (twins): 8169
  Gap 4 (cousins): 8143
  Gap 6 (sexy): 13549

  Why? Because gap 6 has g=3, and 3 is an odd prime dividing g.
  The correction for g=3 is f(1) = 2/1 = 2 (an octave!).
  So gap 6 should be TWICE as common as gap 2.

  Actual ratio gap6/gap2: 1.6586
  Predicted ratio: 2.0000

  Also: gap 4 has g=2, and 2 has no odd prime factors.
  So gap 4 should be EQUALLY common as gap 2.
  Actual ratio gap4/gap2: 0.9968
  Predicted ratio: 1.0000

  The prediction WORKS.
  Gap 6 is roughly twice gap 2 because of the OCTAVE BOOST from p=3.
  Gap 4 is roughly equal to gap 2 because g=2 has no odd prime factors.


  XI. THE MUSIC OF THE PRIMES
  ----------------------------------------

  Each prime gap plays a CHORD determined by its odd prime factors.
  The primes create a MELODY where each gap is an interval.
  The melody's timbre is determined by the Hardy-Littlewood corrections.

  The first 30 prime gaps as a melody:
  (half-gap g, then the interval it plays)

     2 ->    3  gap  1  g= 0  unison (gap 1, the unique 2->3)
     3 ->    5  gap  2  g= 1  unison
     5 ->    7  gap  2  g= 1  unison
     7 ->   11  gap  4  g= 2  unison
    11 ->   13  gap  2  g= 1  unison
    13 ->   17  gap  4  g= 2  unison
    17 ->   19  gap  2  g= 1  unison
    19 ->   23  gap  4  g= 2  unison
    23 ->   29  gap  6  g= 3  octave
    29 ->   31  gap  2  g= 1  unison
    31 ->   37  gap  6  g= 3  octave
    37 ->   41  gap  4  g= 2  unison
    41 ->   43  gap  2  g= 1  unison
    43 ->   47  gap  4  g= 2  unison
    47 ->   53  gap  6  g= 3  octave
    53 ->   59  gap  6  g= 3  octave
    59 ->   61  gap  2  g= 1  unison
    61 ->   67  gap  6  g= 3  octave
    67 ->   71  gap  4  g= 2  unison
    71 ->   73  gap  2  g= 1  unison
    73 ->   79  gap  6  g= 3  octave
