   101 ->  103  gap  2  g= 1  unison
   103 ->  107  gap  4  g= 2  unison
   107 ->  109  gap  2  g= 1  unison
   109 ->  113  gap  4  g= 2  unison
   113 ->  127  gap 14  g= 7  minor third

  The melody: unison, octave, octave, fourth+octave, octave, ...
  It plays in the harmonic series of the primes.
  The most common notes are octaves (from factor 3).
  The rarest notes are unisons (twin primes, g=power of 2).


  XII. THE FORMULA, FINAL FORM
  ----------------------------------------

  pi_{2g}(N) ~ C_2 * CHORD(g) * Li_2(N)

  where:
  C_2 = 2 * prod_{p>=3} (1 - 1/(p-1)^2) ~ 1.3203
  CHORD(g) = prod_{p|g, p odd} (p-1)/(p-2)
           = prod_{p|g, p odd} f(p-2)
           = product of musical intervals for primes dividing g
  Li_2(N) = integral of 1/(ln t)^2 dt from 2 to N

  The CHORD function is MULTIPLICATIVE: CHORD(ab) = CHORD(a)*CHORD(b)
  when gcd(a,b)=1.

  It is the PRODUCT of the superparticular homomorphism f
  evaluated at (prime - 2) for each odd prime dividing g.

  It IS our homomorphism. It IS the formal group.
  The distribution of prime gaps IS governed by the same
  algebraic structure that governs tournaments, music,
  rapidity, and the Cayley transform.

  The primes don't just sit on the rapidity line.
  Their GAPS play CHORDS on it.
  And the chords are determined by the same function f(n) = (n+1)/n
  that is the homomorphism of the formal group,
  that gives the musical intervals,
  that gives the alkane series,
  that gives the polygon sequence,
  that gives the dihedral orders,
  that gives the Cayley transform of consecutive pairs.

  One function. Everywhere.
